t for those who cannot live at home | | **Acute Rehabilitation** | Intensive therapy ≥3 h/day post-acute event | This patient — needing ADL help, mild dementia, no skilled nursing needs — fits **ALF** criteria. ALFs are primarily **private pay** or **Medicaid** (in some states); Medicare does NOT cover ALF costs. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old male named Albert requires assistance with 4 ADLs (bathing, dressing, meals, toileting), has mild dementia with no behavioral disturbances, no complex medical needs, no IV therapy, and no daily skilled nursing requirements. He family is exploring long-term residential care options. Which of the following care settings is most appropriate for this patient''s current level of need?', '["Acute rehabilitation hospital, which is designed for patients requiring intensive physical and occupational therapy (minimum 3 hours/day) following an acute medical event", "Assisted living facility (ALF), which provides 24-hour supervision, personal care (ADL assistance), meals, housekeeping, and social programming for individuals who need help with ADLs but do not require continuous skilled nursing care", "Inpatient psychiatric hospital, which is appropriate because mild dementia with ADL limitations is classified as a psychiatric emergency requiring inpatient stabilization", "Skilled nursing facility (SNF), which is specifically indicated for patients requiring daily skilled nursing care (wound care, IV medications, complex rehabilitation) and would be over-provision for this patient''s current needs", "Independent living retirement community, which requires residents to manage all ADLs independently without assistance and is therefore not appropriate for this patient"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Understanding the **continuum of long-term care settings** is critical for USMLE Step 2 CK: | Setting | Key Criteria | | --- | --- | | **Independent Living (IL)** | Fully independent in ADLs; no care provided | | **Assisted Living (ALF)** | ADL assistance, supervision, no continuous skilled nursing needed | | **Memory Care Unit** | ALF-level care + secure environment for dementia/wandering | | **Skilled Nursing Facility (SNF)** | Daily skilled nursing care, post-acute rehabilitation, complex medical needs | | **Long-Term Care (LTC) / Nursing Home** | Permanent placement for those who cannot live at home | | **Acute Rehabilitation** | Intensive therapy ≥3 h/day post-acute event | This patient — needing ADL help, mild dementia, no skilled nursing needs — fits **ALF** criteria. ALFs are primarily **private pay** or **Medicaid** (in some states); Medicare does NOT cover ALF costs. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old female named Louise requires assistance with 6 ADLs (bathing, dressing, meals, toileting), has mild dementia with no behavioral disturbances, no complex medical needs, no IV therapy, and no daily skilled nursing requirements. She family is exploring long-term residential care options. Which of the following care settings is most appropriate for this patient''s current level of need?', '["Assisted living facility (ALF), which provides 24-hour supervision, personal care (ADL assistance), meals, housekeeping, and social programming for individuals who need help with ADLs but do not require continuous skilled nursing care", "Acute rehabilitation hospital, which is designed for patients requiring intensive physical and occupational therapy (minimum 3 hours/day) following an acute medical event", "Independent living retirement community, which requires residents to manage all ADLs independently without assistance and is therefore not appropriate for this patient", "Inpatient psychiatric hospital, which is appropriate because mild dementia with ADL limitations is classified as a psychiatric emergency requiring inpatient stabilization", "Skilled nursing facility (SNF), which is specifically indicated for patients requiring daily skilled nursing care (wound care, IV medications, complex rehabilitation) and would be over-provision for this patient''s current needs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Understanding the **continuum of long-term care settings** is critical for USMLE Step 2 CK: | Setting | Key Criteria | | --- | --- | | **Independent Living (IL)** | Fully independent in ADLs; no care provided | | **Assisted Living (ALF)** | ADL assistance, supervision, no continuous skilled nursing needed | | **Memory Care Unit** | ALF-level care + secure environment for dementia/wandering | | **Skilled Nursing Facility (SNF)** | Daily skilled nursing care, post-acute rehabilitation, complex medical needs | | **Long-Term Care (LTC) / Nursing Home** | Permanent placement for those who cannot live at home | | **Acute Rehabilitation** | Intensive therapy ≥3 h/day post-acute event | This patient — needing ADL help, mild dementia, no skilled nursing needs — fits **ALF** criteria. ALFs are primarily **private pay** or **Medicaid** (in some states); Medicare does NOT cover ALF costs. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old male named Albert requires permanent nursing home placement. He has $180,000 in savings and a monthly Social Security income of $880/month Social Security Disability. He family asks how nursing home care will be financed and when public assistance becomes available. Which of the following most accurately describes how nursing home care is typically financed in the United States?', '["Medicare Part B covers all nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit, asset requirements, or spend-down obligations", "Nursing home care is completely free of charge for all Americans aged \u2265 65 years old as a standard Medicare benefit with no limitations or eligibility requirements", "Private health insurance (not Medicare or Medicaid) is the primary payer for all long-term nursing home care in the United States", "Nursing home care is initially financed by Medicare Part A (for post-acute skilled care only, limited to 100 days), then private pay using personal assets, and finally by Medicaid once the individual has spent down assets to the state-specified threshold (typically $2,000 for an individual); Medicaid then covers long-term custodial nursing home costs", "Social Security income alone is sufficient to cover all nursing home costs, and no additional private payment or Medicaid enrollment is required"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Nursing home financing** follows a predictable cascade — a critical concept for Step 2 CK: (1) **Medicare Part A**: covers SNF care ONLY after a qualifying hospital stay of ≥3 days; covers days 1–20 at 100%; days 21–100 with a coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); **stops after 100 days** (or when the patient no longer needs skilled care); (2) **Private pay**: patient and family pay out-of-pocket using personal assets (median nursing home cost: ~$9,000–$10,000/month); (3) **Medicaid**: after personal assets are spent down to state threshold (~$2,000 for an individual; ''community spouse resource allowance'' protects some assets for remaining-at-home spouse); Medicaid then covers custodial nursing home care permanently. Key distinction: **Medicare covers skilled care; Medicaid covers custodial/long-term care**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old female named Clara requires permanent nursing home placement. She has $180,000 in savings and a monthly Social Security income of $1,100/month combined income. She family asks how nursing home care will be financed and when public assistance becomes available. Which of the following most accurately describes how nursing home care is typically financed in the United States?', '["Nursing home care is initially financed by Medicare Part A (for post-acute skilled care only, limited to 100 days), then private pay using personal assets, and finally by Medicaid once the individual has spent down assets to the state-specified threshold (typically $2,000 for an individual); Medicaid then covers long-term custodial nursing home costs", "Medicare Part B covers all nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit, asset requirements, or spend-down obligations", "Social Security income alone is sufficient to cover all nursing home costs, and no additional private payment or Medicaid enrollment is required", "Private health insurance (not Medicare or Medicaid) is the primary payer for all long-term nursing home care in the United States", "Nursing home care is completely free of charge for all Americans aged \u2265 65 years old as a standard Medicare benefit with no limitations or eligibility requirements"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Nursing home financing** follows a predictable cascade — a critical concept for Step 2 CK: (1) **Medicare Part A**: covers SNF care ONLY after a qualifying hospital stay of ≥3 days; covers days 1–20 at 100%; days 21–100 with a coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); **stops after 100 days** (or when the patient no longer needs skilled care); (2) **Private pay**: patient and family pay out-of-pocket using personal assets (median nursing home cost: ~$9,000–$10,000/month); (3) **Medicaid**: after personal assets are spent down to state threshold (~$2,000 for an individual; ''community spouse resource allowance'' protects some assets for remaining-at-home spouse); Medicaid then covers custodial nursing home care permanently. Key distinction: **Medicare covers skilled care; Medicaid covers custodial/long-term care**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old female named Helen requires permanent nursing home placement. She has $180,000 in savings and a monthly Social Security income of $1,050/month pension. She family asks how nursing home care will be financed and when public assistance becomes available. Which of the following most accurately describes how nursing home care is typically financed in the United States?', '["Social Security income alone is sufficient to cover all nursing home costs, and no additional private payment or Medicaid enrollment is required", "Nursing home care is initially financed by Medicare Part A (for post-acute skilled care only, limited to 100 days), then private pay using personal assets, and finally by Medicaid once the individual has spent down assets to the state-specified threshold (typically $2,000 for an individual); Medicaid then covers long-term custodial nursing home costs", "Private health insurance (not Medicare or Medicaid) is the primary payer for all long-term nursing home care in the United States", "Nursing home care is completely free of charge for all Americans aged \u2265 65 years old as a standard Medicare benefit with no limitations or eligibility requirements", "Medicare Part B covers all nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit, asset requirements, or spend-down obligations"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Nursing home financing** follows a predictable cascade — a critical concept for Step 2 CK: (1) **Medicare Part A**: covers SNF care ONLY after a qualifying hospital stay of ≥3 days; covers days 1–20 at 100%; days 21–100 with a coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); **stops after 100 days** (or when the patient no longer needs skilled care); (2) **Private pay**: patient and family pay out-of-pocket using personal assets (median nursing home cost: ~$9,000–$10,000/month); (3) **Medicaid**: after personal assets are spent down to state threshold (~$2,000 for an individual; ''community spouse resource allowance'' protects some assets for remaining-at-home spouse); Medicaid then covers custodial nursing home care permanently. Key distinction: **Medicare covers skilled care; Medicaid covers custodial/long-term care**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old female named Catherine requires permanent nursing home placement. She has $180,000 in savings and a monthly Social Security income of $1,100/month combined income. She family asks how nursing home care will be financed and when public assistance becomes available. Which of the following most accurately describes how nursing home care is typically financed in the United States?', '["Social Security income alone is sufficient to cover all nursing home costs, and no additional private payment or Medicaid enrollment is required", "Nursing home care is completely free of charge for all Americans aged \u2265 65 years old as a standard Medicare benefit with no limitations or eligibility requirements", "Medicare Part B covers all nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit, asset requirements, or spend-down obligations", "Nursing home care is initially financed by Medicare Part A (for post-acute skilled care only, limited to 100 days), then private pay using personal assets, and finally by Medicaid once the individual has spent down assets to the state-specified threshold (typically $2,000 for an individual); Medicaid then covers long-term custodial nursing home costs", "Private health insurance (not Medicare or Medicaid) is the primary payer for all long-term nursing home care in the United States"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Nursing home financing** follows a predictable cascade — a critical concept for Step 2 CK: (1) **Medicare Part A**: covers SNF care ONLY after a qualifying hospital stay of ≥3 days; covers days 1–20 at 100%; days 21–100 with a coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); **stops after 100 days** (or when the patient no longer needs skilled care); (2) **Private pay**: patient and family pay out-of-pocket using personal assets (median nursing home cost: ~$9,000–$10,000/month); (3) **Medicaid**: after personal assets are spent down to state threshold (~$2,000 for an individual; ''community spouse resource allowance'' protects some assets for remaining-at-home spouse); Medicaid then covers custodial nursing home care permanently. Key distinction: **Medicare covers skilled care; Medicaid covers custodial/long-term care**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named Arthur requires permanent nursing home placement. He has $180,000 in savings and a monthly Social Security income of $880/month Social Security Disability. He family asks how nursing home care will be financed and when public assistance becomes available. Which of the following most accurately describes how nursing home care is typically financed in the United States?', '["Social Security income alone is sufficient to cover all nursing home costs, and no additional private payment or Medicaid enrollment is required", "Nursing home care is initially financed by Medicare Part A (for post-acute skilled care only, limited to 100 days), then private pay using personal assets, and finally by Medicaid once the individual has spent down assets to the state-specified threshold (typically $2,000 for an individual); Medicaid then covers long-term custodial nursing home costs", "Medicare Part B covers all nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit, asset requirements, or spend-down obligations", "Private health insurance (not Medicare or Medicaid) is the primary payer for all long-term nursing home care in the United States", "Nursing home care is completely free of charge for all Americans aged \u2265 65 years old as a standard Medicare benefit with no limitations or eligibility requirements"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Nursing home financing** follows a predictable cascade — a critical concept for Step 2 CK: (1) **Medicare Part A**: covers SNF care ONLY after a qualifying hospital stay of ≥3 days; covers days 1–20 at 100%; days 21–100 with a coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); **stops after 100 days** (or when the patient no longer needs skilled care); (2) **Private pay**: patient and family pay out-of-pocket using personal assets (median nursing home cost: ~$9,000–$10,000/month); (3) **Medicaid**: after personal assets are spent down to state threshold (~$2,000 for an individual; ''community spouse resource allowance'' protects some assets for remaining-at-home spouse); Medicaid then covers custodial nursing home care permanently. Key distinction: **Medicare covers skilled care; Medicaid covers custodial/long-term care**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
