    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male named Clarence requires permanent nursing home placement. He has $180,000 in savings and a monthly Social Security income of $1,100/month combined income. He family asks how nursing home care will be financed and when public assistance becomes available. Which of the following most accurately describes how nursing home care is typically financed in the United States?', '["Private health insurance (not Medicare or Medicaid) is the primary payer for all long-term nursing home care in the United States", "Nursing home care is initially financed by Medicare Part A (for post-acute skilled care only, limited to 100 days), then private pay using personal assets, and finally by Medicaid once the individual has spent down assets to the state-specified threshold (typically $2,000 for an individual); Medicaid then covers long-term custodial nursing home costs", "Nursing home care is completely free of charge for all Americans aged \u2265 65 years old as a standard Medicare benefit with no limitations or eligibility requirements", "Social Security income alone is sufficient to cover all nursing home costs, and no additional private payment or Medicaid enrollment is required", "Medicare Part B covers all nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit, asset requirements, or spend-down obligations"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Nursing home financing** follows a predictable cascade — a critical concept for Step 2 CK: (1) **Medicare Part A**: covers SNF care ONLY after a qualifying hospital stay of ≥3 days; covers days 1–20 at 100%; days 21–100 with a coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); **stops after 100 days** (or when the patient no longer needs skilled care); (2) **Private pay**: patient and family pay out-of-pocket using personal assets (median nursing home cost: ~$9,000–$10,000/month); (3) **Medicaid**: after personal assets are spent down to state threshold (~$2,000 for an individual; ''community spouse resource allowance'' protects some assets for remaining-at-home spouse); Medicaid then covers custodial nursing home care permanently. Key distinction: **Medicare covers skilled care; Medicaid covers custodial/long-term care**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old male named David requires permanent nursing home placement. He has $180,000 in savings and a monthly Social Security income of $1,050/month pension. He family asks how nursing home care will be financed and when public assistance becomes available. Which of the following most accurately describes how nursing home care is typically financed in the United States?', '["Nursing home care is completely free of charge for all Americans aged \u2265 65 years old as a standard Medicare benefit with no limitations or eligibility requirements", "Medicare Part B covers all nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit, asset requirements, or spend-down obligations", "Social Security income alone is sufficient to cover all nursing home costs, and no additional private payment or Medicaid enrollment is required", "Nursing home care is initially financed by Medicare Part A (for post-acute skilled care only, limited to 100 days), then private pay using personal assets, and finally by Medicaid once the individual has spent down assets to the state-specified threshold (typically $2,000 for an individual); Medicaid then covers long-term custodial nursing home costs", "Private health insurance (not Medicare or Medicaid) is the primary payer for all long-term nursing home care in the United States"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Nursing home financing** follows a predictable cascade — a critical concept for Step 2 CK: (1) **Medicare Part A**: covers SNF care ONLY after a qualifying hospital stay of ≥3 days; covers days 1–20 at 100%; days 21–100 with a coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); **stops after 100 days** (or when the patient no longer needs skilled care); (2) **Private pay**: patient and family pay out-of-pocket using personal assets (median nursing home cost: ~$9,000–$10,000/month); (3) **Medicaid**: after personal assets are spent down to state threshold (~$2,000 for an individual; ''community spouse resource allowance'' protects some assets for remaining-at-home spouse); Medicaid then covers custodial nursing home care permanently. Key distinction: **Medicare covers skilled care; Medicaid covers custodial/long-term care**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old male named Thomas with advanced dementia and total ADL dependence is a long-term resident of a skilled nursing facility. During a clinical visit, you review the facility''s quality indicators and note a high rate of new pressure injuries, frequent use of physical restraints, a high incidence of urinary tract infections, and multiple hospitalizations in the past quarter. What is the most appropriate clinical and advocacy response?', '["These represent evidence of poor nursing home quality; discuss findings with the nursing home medical director and director of nursing, report concerns to the State Survey Agency (or long-term care ombudsman), review individual patient care plans, and advocate for evidence-based care improvements", "Immediately transfer all patients to other facilities without completing a formal investigation, as this is the fastest way to protect patients", "Accept these indicators as normal and expected for an advanced dementia population; document them in the chart without any further advocacy", "Reassure the family that federal regulations do not apply to private-pay nursing home residents, and therefore quality indicators are irrelevant for this patient", "Report the findings exclusively to the nursing home''s marketing department for internal quality improvement without involving any external regulatory bodies"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Nursing home quality indicators** are publicly reported on Medicare''s **Care Compare** (formerly Nursing Home Compare) website. Key quality measures include: (1) **Pressure injury rate** (new/worsening); (2) **Physical restraint use rate** (should be minimized; OBRA 1987 mandates restraint reduction); (3) **Urinary tract infection rate**; (4) **Potentially avoidable hospitalizations**; (5) **Falls with injury rate**; (6) **Antipsychotic use rate** (high use = chemical restraint concern). High rates of these indicators signal **substandard care**. The clinician''s advocacy role: (1) raise concerns with the **Medical Director** and **Director of Nursing**; (2) report to the **State Survey Agency** (conducts unannounced inspections) and/or **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** (advocates for residents'' rights); (3) review individual patient care plans. Federal nursing home regulations (OBRA 1987) mandate minimum quality standards. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17 & Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
