    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old female named Virginia with advanced dementia and total ADL dependence is a long-term resident of a skilled nursing facility. During a clinical visit, you review the facility''s quality indicators and note a high rate of new pressure injuries, frequent use of physical restraints, a high incidence of urinary tract infections, and multiple hospitalizations in the past quarter. What is the most appropriate clinical and advocacy response?', '["Report the findings exclusively to the nursing home''s marketing department for internal quality improvement without involving any external regulatory bodies", "Reassure the family that federal regulations do not apply to private-pay nursing home residents, and therefore quality indicators are irrelevant for this patient", "Immediately transfer all patients to other facilities without completing a formal investigation, as this is the fastest way to protect patients", "These represent evidence of poor nursing home quality; discuss findings with the nursing home medical director and director of nursing, report concerns to the State Survey Agency (or long-term care ombudsman), review individual patient care plans, and advocate for evidence-based care improvements", "Accept these indicators as normal and expected for an advanced dementia population; document them in the chart without any further advocacy"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Nursing home quality indicators** are publicly reported on Medicare''s **Care Compare** (formerly Nursing Home Compare) website. Key quality measures include: (1) **Pressure injury rate** (new/worsening); (2) **Physical restraint use rate** (should be minimized; OBRA 1987 mandates restraint reduction); (3) **Urinary tract infection rate**; (4) **Potentially avoidable hospitalizations**; (5) **Falls with injury rate**; (6) **Antipsychotic use rate** (high use = chemical restraint concern). High rates of these indicators signal **substandard care**. The clinician''s advocacy role: (1) raise concerns with the **Medical Director** and **Director of Nursing**; (2) report to the **State Survey Agency** (conducts unannounced inspections) and/or **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** (advocates for residents'' rights); (3) review individual patient care plans. Federal nursing home regulations (OBRA 1987) mandate minimum quality standards. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17 & Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female named Jean with advanced dementia and total ADL dependence is a long-term resident of a skilled nursing facility. During a clinical visit, you review the facility''s quality indicators and note a high rate of new pressure injuries, frequent use of physical restraints, a high incidence of urinary tract infections, and multiple hospitalizations in the past quarter. What is the most appropriate clinical and advocacy response?', '["Accept these indicators as normal and expected for an advanced dementia population; document them in the chart without any further advocacy", "These represent evidence of poor nursing home quality; discuss findings with the nursing home medical director and director of nursing, report concerns to the State Survey Agency (or long-term care ombudsman), review individual patient care plans, and advocate for evidence-based care improvements", "Immediately transfer all patients to other facilities without completing a formal investigation, as this is the fastest way to protect patients", "Reassure the family that federal regulations do not apply to private-pay nursing home residents, and therefore quality indicators are irrelevant for this patient", "Report the findings exclusively to the nursing home''s marketing department for internal quality improvement without involving any external regulatory bodies"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Nursing home quality indicators** are publicly reported on Medicare''s **Care Compare** (formerly Nursing Home Compare) website. Key quality measures include: (1) **Pressure injury rate** (new/worsening); (2) **Physical restraint use rate** (should be minimized; OBRA 1987 mandates restraint reduction); (3) **Urinary tract infection rate**; (4) **Potentially avoidable hospitalizations**; (5) **Falls with injury rate**; (6) **Antipsychotic use rate** (high use = chemical restraint concern). High rates of these indicators signal **substandard care**. The clinician''s advocacy role: (1) raise concerns with the **Medical Director** and **Director of Nursing**; (2) report to the **State Survey Agency** (conducts unannounced inspections) and/or **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** (advocates for residents'' rights); (3) review individual patient care plans. Federal nursing home regulations (OBRA 1987) mandate minimum quality standards. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17 & Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old female named Shirley with severe dementia is cared for at home by her exhausted nephew. Despite home health aide services 12 hours/day, the patient wanders nocturnally, has severe sundowning with agitation requiring physical intervention, is incontinent of bowel and bladder, and has developed a Stage III sacral pressure injury. The nephew has had multiple falls while trying to reposition the patient at night. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical recommendation?', '["Prescribe high-dose quetiapine to eliminate all sundowning behaviors and continue indefinite home care without reassessment", "Transfer the patient to an acute psychiatric inpatient unit for permanent long-term management of behavioral symptoms of dementia", "Recommend the nephew purchase a hospital-style bed with four-point restraints and hire an additional uncertified family friend for nighttime repositioning", "Continue home care at its current level with no changes, as families must never be recommended nursing home placement by a physician", "The patient''s care needs (nocturnal wandering, severe BPSD requiring physical intervention, total incontinence, Stage III pressure injury, caregiver injury risk) now exceed what can be safely managed at home; nursing home placement in a secure memory care unit with 24-hour skilled nursing is clinically indicated"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Nursing home placement** becomes clinically indicated when home care cannot safely meet the patient''s needs despite maximized community supports. Established criteria for nursing home level of care: (1) **Total ADL dependence** with complex skilled nursing needs (wound care, etc.); (2) **Severe behavioral and psychological symptoms of dementia (BPSD)** requiring constant intervention beyond caregiver capacity; (3) **Nocturnal wandering** that cannot be safely managed at home; (4) **Caregiver safety**: caregiver injury from patient care is a critical threshold indicator; (5) **Complex medical needs**: Stage III/IV pressure injuries, tube feeding, IV medications. The clinician''s role is to have an honest, compassionate conversation about the patient''s evolving care needs and the limits of home-based capacity — framing nursing home placement as meeting the patient''s clinical needs, NOT as abandonment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old female named Phyllis with severe dementia is cared for at home by her exhausted paid caregiver. Despite home health aide services 12 hours/day, the patient wanders nocturnally, has severe sundowning with agitation requiring physical intervention, is incontinent of bowel and bladder, and has developed a Stage III sacral pressure injury. The paid caregiver has had multiple falls while trying to reposition the patient at night. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical recommendation?', '["Recommend the paid caregiver purchase a hospital-style bed with four-point restraints and hire an additional uncertified family friend for nighttime repositioning", "Continue home care at its current level with no changes, as families must never be recommended nursing home placement by a physician", "Transfer the patient to an acute psychiatric inpatient unit for permanent long-term management of behavioral symptoms of dementia", "Prescribe high-dose quetiapine to eliminate all sundowning behaviors and continue indefinite home care without reassessment", "The patient''s care needs (nocturnal wandering, severe BPSD requiring physical intervention, total incontinence, Stage III pressure injury, caregiver injury risk) now exceed what can be safely managed at home; nursing home placement in a secure memory care unit with 24-hour skilled nursing is clinically indicated"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Nursing home placement** becomes clinically indicated when home care cannot safely meet the patient''s needs despite maximized community supports. Established criteria for nursing home level of care: (1) **Total ADL dependence** with complex skilled nursing needs (wound care, etc.); (2) **Severe behavioral and psychological symptoms of dementia (BPSD)** requiring constant intervention beyond caregiver capacity; (3) **Nocturnal wandering** that cannot be safely managed at home; (4) **Caregiver safety**: caregiver injury from patient care is a critical threshold indicator; (5) **Complex medical needs**: Stage III/IV pressure injuries, tube feeding, IV medications. The clinician''s role is to have an honest, compassionate conversation about the patient''s evolving care needs and the limits of home-based capacity — framing nursing home placement as meeting the patient''s clinical needs, NOT as abandonment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
