    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female named Irene with severe dementia is cared for at home by her exhausted nephew. Despite home health aide services 14 hours/day, the patient wanders nocturnally, has severe sundowning with agitation requiring physical intervention, is incontinent of bowel and bladder, and has developed a Stage III sacral pressure injury. The nephew has had multiple falls while trying to reposition the patient at night. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical recommendation?', '["Continue home care at its current level with no changes, as families must never be recommended nursing home placement by a physician", "Transfer the patient to an acute psychiatric inpatient unit for permanent long-term management of behavioral symptoms of dementia", "Recommend the nephew purchase a hospital-style bed with four-point restraints and hire an additional uncertified family friend for nighttime repositioning", "Prescribe high-dose quetiapine to eliminate all sundowning behaviors and continue indefinite home care without reassessment", "The patient''s care needs (nocturnal wandering, severe BPSD requiring physical intervention, total incontinence, Stage III pressure injury, caregiver injury risk) now exceed what can be safely managed at home; nursing home placement in a secure memory care unit with 24-hour skilled nursing is clinically indicated"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Nursing home placement** becomes clinically indicated when home care cannot safely meet the patient''s needs despite maximized community supports. Established criteria for nursing home level of care: (1) **Total ADL dependence** with complex skilled nursing needs (wound care, etc.); (2) **Severe behavioral and psychological symptoms of dementia (BPSD)** requiring constant intervention beyond caregiver capacity; (3) **Nocturnal wandering** that cannot be safely managed at home; (4) **Caregiver safety**: caregiver injury from patient care is a critical threshold indicator; (5) **Complex medical needs**: Stage III/IV pressure injuries, tube feeding, IV medications. The clinician''s role is to have an honest, compassionate conversation about the patient''s evolving care needs and the limits of home-based capacity — framing nursing home placement as meeting the patient''s clinical needs, NOT as abandonment. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 4; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old male named Harry was admitted to the hospital for 12 days following an ischemic stroke with left hemiplegia. He needs intensive physical therapy, occupational therapy, and speech therapy to regain function, along with daily skilled nursing assessment. He is not yet stable enough for home discharge. The medical team discusses post-acute placement options. Which of the following best describes the appropriate placement and its Medicare coverage?', '["Transfer to a skilled nursing facility (SNF) for post-acute rehabilitation and skilled nursing care; Medicare Part A covers SNF care at 100% for days 1\u201320 after a qualifying hospital stay of \u22653 days, with cost-sharing from days 21\u2013100", "Discharge to a long-term care (LTC) custodial nursing home; Medicare Part A covers all custodial nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit", "Admission to an acute inpatient psychiatric hospital for stroke rehabilitation, as neurological events are classified as psychiatric emergencies under Medicare guidelines", "Transfer to an assisted living facility (ALF) for post-stroke rehabilitation; Medicare Part A fully covers all ALF costs for up to 2 years after a qualifying hospital stay", "Direct home discharge with Medicare Part A-funded 24-hour home nursing care for 6 months to complete rehabilitation"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Skilled nursing facility (SNF) care** vs. **long-term custodial care** is a critical distinction for USMLE Step 2 CK and geriatric practice: **SNF (post-acute care)**: Provides skilled nursing + rehabilitation (PT, OT, ST) for patients recovering from acute illness, surgery, or stroke. **Medicare Part A coverage**: 100% for days 1–20 (after ≥3-day qualifying hospital stay); days 21–100: patient pays coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); after day 100: patient pays 100%. **Qualifying criteria**: (1) 3-day inpatient hospital stay; (2) admission within 30 days of discharge; (3) daily skilled care needed. **LTC (custodial care)**: Provides personal care and supervision for patients who cannot safely live at home. Medicare does NOT cover long-term custodial nursing home care; this is primarily funded by Medicaid (after spend-down) or private pay. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old male named Frank was admitted to the hospital for 3 days following an ischemic stroke with left hemiplegia. He needs intensive physical therapy, occupational therapy, and speech therapy to regain function, along with daily skilled nursing assessment. He is not yet stable enough for home discharge. The medical team discusses post-acute placement options. Which of the following best describes the appropriate placement and its Medicare coverage?', '["Admission to an acute inpatient psychiatric hospital for stroke rehabilitation, as neurological events are classified as psychiatric emergencies under Medicare guidelines", "Direct home discharge with Medicare Part A-funded 24-hour home nursing care for 6 months to complete rehabilitation", "Transfer to an assisted living facility (ALF) for post-stroke rehabilitation; Medicare Part A fully covers all ALF costs for up to 2 years after a qualifying hospital stay", "Transfer to a skilled nursing facility (SNF) for post-acute rehabilitation and skilled nursing care; Medicare Part A covers SNF care at 100% for days 1\u201320 after a qualifying hospital stay of \u22653 days, with cost-sharing from days 21\u2013100", "Discharge to a long-term care (LTC) custodial nursing home; Medicare Part A covers all custodial nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Skilled nursing facility (SNF) care** vs. **long-term custodial care** is a critical distinction for USMLE Step 2 CK and geriatric practice: **SNF (post-acute care)**: Provides skilled nursing + rehabilitation (PT, OT, ST) for patients recovering from acute illness, surgery, or stroke. **Medicare Part A coverage**: 100% for days 1–20 (after ≥3-day qualifying hospital stay); days 21–100: patient pays coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); after day 100: patient pays 100%. **Qualifying criteria**: (1) 3-day inpatient hospital stay; (2) admission within 30 days of discharge; (3) daily skilled care needed. **LTC (custodial care)**: Provides personal care and supervision for patients who cannot safely live at home. Medicare does NOT cover long-term custodial nursing home care; this is primarily funded by Medicaid (after spend-down) or private pay. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old female named Phyllis was admitted to the hospital for 9 days following an ischemic stroke with left hemiplegia. She needs intensive physical therapy, occupational therapy, and speech therapy to regain function, along with daily skilled nursing assessment. She is not yet stable enough for home discharge. The medical team discusses post-acute placement options. Which of the following best describes the appropriate placement and its Medicare coverage?', '["Discharge to a long-term care (LTC) custodial nursing home; Medicare Part A covers all custodial nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit", "Direct home discharge with Medicare Part A-funded 24-hour home nursing care for 6 months to complete rehabilitation", "Transfer to an assisted living facility (ALF) for post-stroke rehabilitation; Medicare Part A fully covers all ALF costs for up to 2 years after a qualifying hospital stay", "Transfer to a skilled nursing facility (SNF) for post-acute rehabilitation and skilled nursing care; Medicare Part A covers SNF care at 100% for days 1\u201320 after a qualifying hospital stay of \u22653 days, with cost-sharing from days 21\u2013100", "Admission to an acute inpatient psychiatric hospital for stroke rehabilitation, as neurological events are classified as psychiatric emergencies under Medicare guidelines"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Skilled nursing facility (SNF) care** vs. **long-term custodial care** is a critical distinction for USMLE Step 2 CK and geriatric practice: **SNF (post-acute care)**: Provides skilled nursing + rehabilitation (PT, OT, ST) for patients recovering from acute illness, surgery, or stroke. **Medicare Part A coverage**: 100% for days 1–20 (after ≥3-day qualifying hospital stay); days 21–100: patient pays coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); after day 100: patient pays 100%. **Qualifying criteria**: (1) 3-day inpatient hospital stay; (2) admission within 30 days of discharge; (3) daily skilled care needed. **LTC (custodial care)**: Provides personal care and supervision for patients who cannot safely live at home. Medicare does NOT cover long-term custodial nursing home care; this is primarily funded by Medicaid (after spend-down) or private pay. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female named Irene was admitted to the hospital for 5 days following an ischemic stroke with left hemiplegia. She needs intensive physical therapy, occupational therapy, and speech therapy to regain function, along with daily skilled nursing assessment. She is not yet stable enough for home discharge. The medical team discusses post-acute placement options. Which of the following best describes the appropriate placement and its Medicare coverage?', '["Direct home discharge with Medicare Part A-funded 24-hour home nursing care for 6 months to complete rehabilitation", "Discharge to a long-term care (LTC) custodial nursing home; Medicare Part A covers all custodial nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit", "Transfer to a skilled nursing facility (SNF) for post-acute rehabilitation and skilled nursing care; Medicare Part A covers SNF care at 100% for days 1\u201320 after a qualifying hospital stay of \u22653 days, with cost-sharing from days 21\u2013100", "Admission to an acute inpatient psychiatric hospital for stroke rehabilitation, as neurological events are classified as psychiatric emergencies under Medicare guidelines", "Transfer to an assisted living facility (ALF) for post-stroke rehabilitation; Medicare Part A fully covers all ALF costs for up to 2 years after a qualifying hospital stay"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Skilled nursing facility (SNF) care** vs. **long-term custodial care** is a critical distinction for USMLE Step 2 CK and geriatric practice: **SNF (post-acute care)**: Provides skilled nursing + rehabilitation (PT, OT, ST) for patients recovering from acute illness, surgery, or stroke. **Medicare Part A coverage**: 100% for days 1–20 (after ≥3-day qualifying hospital stay); days 21–100: patient pays coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); after day 100: patient pays 100%. **Qualifying criteria**: (1) 3-day inpatient hospital stay; (2) admission within 30 days of discharge; (3) daily skilled care needed. **LTC (custod