l care)**: Provides personal care and supervision for patients who cannot safely live at home. Medicare does NOT cover long-term custodial nursing home care; this is primarily funded by Medicaid (after spend-down) or private pay. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male named David was admitted to the hospital for 5 days following an ischemic stroke with left hemiplegia. He needs intensive physical therapy, occupational therapy, and speech therapy to regain function, along with daily skilled nursing assessment. He is not yet stable enough for home discharge. The medical team discusses post-acute placement options. Which of the following best describes the appropriate placement and its Medicare coverage?', '["Transfer to an assisted living facility (ALF) for post-stroke rehabilitation; Medicare Part A fully covers all ALF costs for up to 2 years after a qualifying hospital stay", "Transfer to a skilled nursing facility (SNF) for post-acute rehabilitation and skilled nursing care; Medicare Part A covers SNF care at 100% for days 1\u201320 after a qualifying hospital stay of \u22653 days, with cost-sharing from days 21\u2013100", "Direct home discharge with Medicare Part A-funded 24-hour home nursing care for 6 months to complete rehabilitation", "Discharge to a long-term care (LTC) custodial nursing home; Medicare Part A covers all custodial nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit", "Admission to an acute inpatient psychiatric hospital for stroke rehabilitation, as neurological events are classified as psychiatric emergencies under Medicare guidelines"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Skilled nursing facility (SNF) care** vs. **long-term custodial care** is a critical distinction for USMLE Step 2 CK and geriatric practice: **SNF (post-acute care)**: Provides skilled nursing + rehabilitation (PT, OT, ST) for patients recovering from acute illness, surgery, or stroke. **Medicare Part A coverage**: 100% for days 1–20 (after ≥3-day qualifying hospital stay); days 21–100: patient pays coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); after day 100: patient pays 100%. **Qualifying criteria**: (1) 3-day inpatient hospital stay; (2) admission within 30 days of discharge; (3) daily skilled care needed. **LTC (custodial care)**: Provides personal care and supervision for patients who cannot safely live at home. Medicare does NOT cover long-term custodial nursing home care; this is primarily funded by Medicaid (after spend-down) or private pay. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old male named Leonard was admitted to the hospital for 6 days following an ischemic stroke with left hemiplegia. He needs intensive physical therapy, occupational therapy, and speech therapy to regain function, along with daily skilled nursing assessment. He is not yet stable enough for home discharge. The medical team discusses post-acute placement options. Which of the following best describes the appropriate placement and its Medicare coverage?', '["Direct home discharge with Medicare Part A-funded 24-hour home nursing care for 6 months to complete rehabilitation", "Transfer to an assisted living facility (ALF) for post-stroke rehabilitation; Medicare Part A fully covers all ALF costs for up to 2 years after a qualifying hospital stay", "Admission to an acute inpatient psychiatric hospital for stroke rehabilitation, as neurological events are classified as psychiatric emergencies under Medicare guidelines", "Discharge to a long-term care (LTC) custodial nursing home; Medicare Part A covers all custodial nursing home costs permanently without any duration limit", "Transfer to a skilled nursing facility (SNF) for post-acute rehabilitation and skilled nursing care; Medicare Part A covers SNF care at 100% for days 1\u201320 after a qualifying hospital stay of \u22653 days, with cost-sharing from days 21\u2013100"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Skilled nursing facility (SNF) care** vs. **long-term custodial care** is a critical distinction for USMLE Step 2 CK and geriatric practice: **SNF (post-acute care)**: Provides skilled nursing + rehabilitation (PT, OT, ST) for patients recovering from acute illness, surgery, or stroke. **Medicare Part A coverage**: 100% for days 1–20 (after ≥3-day qualifying hospital stay); days 21–100: patient pays coinsurance ($194.50/day in 2024); after day 100: patient pays 100%. **Qualifying criteria**: (1) 3-day inpatient hospital stay; (2) admission within 30 days of discharge; (3) daily skilled care needed. **LTC (custodial care)**: Provides personal care and supervision for patients who cannot safely live at home. Medicare does NOT cover long-term custodial nursing home care; this is primarily funded by Medicaid (after spend-down) or private pay. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old female named Norma with Parkinson''s disease meets nursing home level of care criteria but wishes to remain at home. She has limited income and assets and is Medicaid eligible. The social worker mentions that Norma may qualify for a program that uses Medicaid funds to purchase home-based services as an alternative to nursing home placement. Which of the following programs is being described?', '["The Medicaid Disproportionate Share Hospital (DSH) program, which provides financial support to hospitals serving large numbers of low-income patients but does not fund home care services", "Medicaid Home and Community-Based Services (HCBS) 1915(c) waiver program, which allows states to use Medicaid funds to provide personal care aides, adult day health, respite care, home modifications, and case management as alternatives to nursing home placement", "Medicare Part C (Medicare Advantage), which replaces all Medicaid benefits and provides comprehensive home care services for dually eligible beneficiaries", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit program, which provides a home health aide for 365 days per year for all homebound Medicare beneficiaries", "The Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act (COBRA), which allows individuals to continue employer-sponsored health insurance after job loss but does not cover home care services"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Medicaid HCBS (Home and Community-Based Services) 1915(c) waivers** are state-administered programs authorized under Section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act that allow states to use Medicaid funds — typically reserved for institutional care — to provide community-based services as alternatives to nursing home placement. Waiver services typically include: (1) **Personal care aides** (ADL assistance); (2) **Home health aide services**; (3) **Adult day health programs**; (4) **Respite care**; (5) **Home modifications** (ramps, grab bars); (6) **Case management**; (7) **Meal services**. Eligibility: Medicaid-eligible individuals who meet nursing home level of care. Key point: HCBS waivers explicitly aim to honor the ADA Olmstead Supreme Court decision (1999), which mandates that states provide services in the least restrictive appropriate setting (community vs. institution). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old female named Esther with Parkinson''s disease meets nursing home level of care criteria but wishes to remain at home. She has limited income and assets and is Medicaid eligible. The social worker mentions that Esther may qualify for a program that uses Medicaid funds to purchase home-based services as an alternative to nursing home placement. Which of the following programs is being described?', '["The Medicaid Disproportionate Share Hospital (DSH) program, which provides financial support to hospitals serving large numbers of low-income patients but does not fund home care services", "The Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act (COBRA), which allows individuals to continue employer-sponsored health insurance after job loss but does not cover home care services", "Medicaid Home and Community-Based Services (HCBS) 1915(c) waiver program, which allows states to use Medicaid funds to provide personal care aides, adult day health, respite care, home modifications, and case management as alternatives to nursing home placement", "Medicare Part C (Medicare Advantage), which replaces all Medicaid benefits and provides comprehensive home care services for dually eligible beneficiaries", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit program, which provides a home health aide for 365 days per year for all homebound Medicare beneficiaries"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Medicaid HCBS (Home and Community-Based Services) 1915(c) waivers** are state-administered programs authorized under Section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act that allow states to use Medicaid funds — typically reserved for institutional care — to provide community-based services as alternatives to nursing home placement. Waiver services typically include: (1) **Personal care aides** (ADL assistance); (2) **Home health aide services**; (3) **Adult day health programs**; (4) **Respite care**; (5) **Home modifications** (ramps, grab bars); (6) **Case management**; (7) **Meal services**. Eligibility: Medicaid-eligible individuals who meet nursing home level of care. Key point: HCBS waivers explicitly aim to honor the ADA Olmstead Supreme Court decision (1999), which mandates that states provide services in the least restrictive appropriate setting (community vs. institution). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
