    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Blanche with Parkinson''s disease meets nursing home level of care criteria but wishes to remain at home. She has limited income and assets and is Medicaid eligible. The social worker mentions that Blanche may qualify for a program that uses Medicaid funds to purchase home-based services as an alternative to nursing home placement. Which of the following programs is being described?', '["Medicare Part C (Medicare Advantage), which replaces all Medicaid benefits and provides comprehensive home care services for dually eligible beneficiaries", "Medicaid Home and Community-Based Services (HCBS) 1915(c) waiver program, which allows states to use Medicaid funds to provide personal care aides, adult day health, respite care, home modifications, and case management as alternatives to nursing home placement", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit program, which provides a home health aide for 365 days per year for all homebound Medicare beneficiaries", "The Medicaid Disproportionate Share Hospital (DSH) program, which provides financial support to hospitals serving large numbers of low-income patients but does not fund home care services", "The Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act (COBRA), which allows individuals to continue employer-sponsored health insurance after job loss but does not cover home care services"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Medicaid HCBS (Home and Community-Based Services) 1915(c) waivers** are state-administered programs authorized under Section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act that allow states to use Medicaid funds — typically reserved for institutional care — to provide community-based services as alternatives to nursing home placement. Waiver services typically include: (1) **Personal care aides** (ADL assistance); (2) **Home health aide services**; (3) **Adult day health programs**; (4) **Respite care**; (5) **Home modifications** (ramps, grab bars); (6) **Case management**; (7) **Meal services**. Eligibility: Medicaid-eligible individuals who meet nursing home level of care. Key point: HCBS waivers explicitly aim to honor the ADA Olmstead Supreme Court decision (1999), which mandates that states provide services in the least restrictive appropriate setting (community vs. institution). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old female named Martha with Parkinson''s disease meets nursing home level of care criteria but wishes to remain at home. She has limited income and assets and is Medicaid eligible. The social worker mentions that Martha may qualify for a program that uses Medicaid funds to purchase home-based services as an alternative to nursing home placement. Which of the following programs is being described?', '["Medicaid Home and Community-Based Services (HCBS) 1915(c) waiver program, which allows states to use Medicaid funds to provide personal care aides, adult day health, respite care, home modifications, and case management as alternatives to nursing home placement", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit program, which provides a home health aide for 365 days per year for all homebound Medicare beneficiaries", "The Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act (COBRA), which allows individuals to continue employer-sponsored health insurance after job loss but does not cover home care services", "Medicare Part C (Medicare Advantage), which replaces all Medicaid benefits and provides comprehensive home care services for dually eligible beneficiaries", "The Medicaid Disproportionate Share Hospital (DSH) program, which provides financial support to hospitals serving large numbers of low-income patients but does not fund home care services"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Medicaid HCBS (Home and Community-Based Services) 1915(c) waivers** are state-administered programs authorized under Section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act that allow states to use Medicaid funds — typically reserved for institutional care — to provide community-based services as alternatives to nursing home placement. Waiver services typically include: (1) **Personal care aides** (ADL assistance); (2) **Home health aide services**; (3) **Adult day health programs**; (4) **Respite care**; (5) **Home modifications** (ramps, grab bars); (6) **Case management**; (7) **Meal services**. Eligibility: Medicaid-eligible individuals who meet nursing home level of care. Key point: HCBS waivers explicitly aim to honor the ADA Olmstead Supreme Court decision (1999), which mandates that states provide services in the least restrictive appropriate setting (community vs. institution). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
