id-eligible individuals who meet nursing home level of care. Key point: HCBS waivers explicitly aim to honor the ADA Olmstead Supreme Court decision (1999), which mandates that states provide services in the least restrictive appropriate setting (community vs. institution). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old male named Vernon with Parkinson''s disease meets nursing home level of care criteria but wishes to remain at home. He has limited income and assets and is Medicaid eligible. The social worker mentions that Vernon may qualify for a program that uses Medicaid funds to purchase home-based services as an alternative to nursing home placement. Which of the following programs is being described?', '["The Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act (COBRA), which allows individuals to continue employer-sponsored health insurance after job loss but does not cover home care services", "The Medicaid Disproportionate Share Hospital (DSH) program, which provides financial support to hospitals serving large numbers of low-income patients but does not fund home care services", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit program, which provides a home health aide for 365 days per year for all homebound Medicare beneficiaries", "Medicaid Home and Community-Based Services (HCBS) 1915(c) waiver program, which allows states to use Medicaid funds to provide personal care aides, adult day health, respite care, home modifications, and case management as alternatives to nursing home placement", "Medicare Part C (Medicare Advantage), which replaces all Medicaid benefits and provides comprehensive home care services for dually eligible beneficiaries"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Medicaid HCBS (Home and Community-Based Services) 1915(c) waivers** are state-administered programs authorized under Section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act that allow states to use Medicaid funds — typically reserved for institutional care — to provide community-based services as alternatives to nursing home placement. Waiver services typically include: (1) **Personal care aides** (ADL assistance); (2) **Home health aide services**; (3) **Adult day health programs**; (4) **Respite care**; (5) **Home modifications** (ramps, grab bars); (6) **Case management**; (7) **Meal services**. Eligibility: Medicaid-eligible individuals who meet nursing home level of care. Key point: HCBS waivers explicitly aim to honor the ADA Olmstead Supreme Court decision (1999), which mandates that states provide services in the least restrictive appropriate setting (community vs. institution). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An attorney named Louise advising a geriatric care team in Texas asks about the legal basis for providing community-based care as an alternative to institutionalization for Medicaid-eligible older adults with disabilities. Which of the following landmark legal decisions established the principle that states must provide services to people with disabilities in the most integrated, least restrictive setting appropriate to their needs?', '["Brown v. Board of Education (1954), a Supreme Court civil rights ruling addressing racial segregation in public schools", "Roe v. Wade (1973), a Supreme Court decision related to reproductive rights that has no relevance to the provision of community-based care for older adults", "Olmstead v. L.C. (1999), a U.S. Supreme Court decision interpreting Title II of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), which established that the unjustified institutionalization of persons with disabilities constitutes discrimination and requires states to provide community-based services when clinically appropriate and the individual chooses community placement", "The Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (EMTALA, 1986), which mandates emergency medical screening but does not address long-term residential placement decisions", "The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA, 1996), which protects patient health information privacy but does not govern the setting of care for disabled individuals"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Olmstead v. L.C. (1999)** is the landmark U.S. Supreme Court case (L.C. v. Olmstead, Commissioner, Georgia Department of Human Resources) that interpreted **Title II of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)**. The Court held that: (1) The **unjustified institutionalization** of persons with mental disabilities constitutes **discrimination** under the ADA; (2) States are required to provide community-based services to persons with disabilities when: (a) community placement is appropriate; (b) the transfer is not opposed by the affected individual; (c) community placement can be reasonably accommodated. This decision is the legal foundation for all **HCBS waivers**, **PACE**, and other community-based long-term care programs. It directly shapes Medicaid long-term care policy. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An attorney named Vernon advising a geriatric care team in Florida asks about the legal basis for providing community-based care as an alternative to institutionalization for Medicaid-eligible older adults with disabilities. Which of the following landmark legal decisions established the principle that states must provide services to people with disabilities in the most integrated, least restrictive setting appropriate to their needs?', '["The Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (EMTALA, 1986), which mandates emergency medical screening but does not address long-term residential placement decisions", "Roe v. Wade (1973), a Supreme Court decision related to reproductive rights that has no relevance to the provision of community-based care for older adults", "Brown v. Board of Education (1954), a Supreme Court civil rights ruling addressing racial segregation in public schools", "The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA, 1996), which protects patient health information privacy but does not govern the setting of care for disabled individuals", "Olmstead v. L.C. (1999), a U.S. Supreme Court decision interpreting Title II of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), which established that the unjustified institutionalization of persons with disabilities constitutes discrimination and requires states to provide community-based services when clinically appropriate and the individual chooses community placement"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Olmstead v. L.C. (1999)** is the landmark U.S. Supreme Court case (L.C. v. Olmstead, Commissioner, Georgia Department of Human Resources) that interpreted **Title II of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)**. The Court held that: (1) The **unjustified institutionalization** of persons with mental disabilities constitutes **discrimination** under the ADA; (2) States are required to provide community-based services to persons with disabilities when: (a) community placement is appropriate; (b) the transfer is not opposed by the affected individual; (c) community placement can be reasonably accommodated. This decision is the legal foundation for all **HCBS waivers**, **PACE**, and other community-based long-term care programs. It directly shapes Medicaid long-term care policy. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
