fer is not opposed by the affected individual; (c) community placement can be reasonably accommodated. This decision is the legal foundation for all **HCBS waivers**, **PACE**, and other community-based long-term care programs. It directly shapes Medicaid long-term care policy. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female named Eleanor is newly diagnosed with early Parkinson''s disease and asks her clinician about financial planning for future long-term care needs. The clinician discusses long-term care insurance. Which of the following accurately describes the role and key limitations of traditional long-term care insurance in financing geriatric care?', '["Long-term care insurance policies universally cover 100% of all care costs indefinitely with no caps, exclusions, or waiting periods", "Medicare Part A automatically serves as long-term care insurance and covers all custodial nursing home and assisted living costs for beneficiaries who need them", "Long-term care insurance (LTCI) helps pay for nursing home, assisted living, and home care costs not covered by Medicare; however, premiums are expensive, most policies require medical underwriting (making purchase after diagnosis difficult or impossible), benefits may have daily caps, and only about 7\u20138% of older Americans currently hold LTCI policies", "Long-term care insurance is a mandatory federal benefit for all Americans age 65 and older, funded through Social Security payroll taxes", "Long-term care insurance can be purchased at any age, even with a pre-existing diagnosis of dementia or advanced cancer, at no additional premium cost"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Long-term care insurance (LTCI)** is a private insurance product designed to cover costs associated with nursing home, assisted living, or home care that Medicare does not cover. Key features and limitations: (1) **Medical underwriting**: applicants must qualify based on health status; purchasing after a diagnosis (Parkinson''s, dementia, etc.) is typically impossible or prohibitively expensive; (2) **Premium cost**: can exceed $2,000–$5,000+/year for comprehensive coverage; (3) **Benefit caps**: most policies have daily benefit caps (e.g., $150–$300/day) and limited policy duration (2–5 years is common); (4) **Benefit triggers**: typically triggered by dependence in ≥2 of 6 ADLs or cognitive impairment; (5) **Penetration rate**: very low (~7–8%); the long-term care financing gap is a major national policy challenge. The ideal time to purchase is in one''s 50s–early 60s, while still insurable. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Beatrice is newly diagnosed with early Parkinson''s disease and asks her clinician about financial planning for future long-term care needs. The clinician discusses long-term care insurance. Which of the following accurately describes the role and key limitations of traditional long-term care insurance in financing geriatric care?', '["Long-term care insurance (LTCI) helps pay for nursing home, assisted living, and home care costs not covered by Medicare; however, premiums are expensive, most policies require medical underwriting (making purchase after diagnosis difficult or impossible), benefits may have daily caps, and only about 7\u20138% of older Americans currently hold LTCI policies", "Long-term care insurance policies universally cover 100% of all care costs indefinitely with no caps, exclusions, or waiting periods", "Medicare Part A automatically serves as long-term care insurance and covers all custodial nursing home and assisted living costs for beneficiaries who need them", "Long-term care insurance is a mandatory federal benefit for all Americans age 65 and older, funded through Social Security payroll taxes", "Long-term care insurance can be purchased at any age, even with a pre-existing diagnosis of dementia or advanced cancer, at no additional premium cost"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Long-term care insurance (LTCI)** is a private insurance product designed to cover costs associated with nursing home, assisted living, or home care that Medicare does not cover. Key features and limitations: (1) **Medical underwriting**: applicants must qualify based on health status; purchasing after a diagnosis (Parkinson''s, dementia, etc.) is typically impossible or prohibitively expensive; (2) **Premium cost**: can exceed $2,000–$5,000+/year for comprehensive coverage; (3) **Benefit caps**: most policies have daily benefit caps (e.g., $150–$300/day) and limited policy duration (2–5 years is common); (4) **Benefit triggers**: typically triggered by dependence in ≥2 of 6 ADLs or cognitive impairment; (5) **Penetration rate**: very low (~7–8%); the long-term care financing gap is a major national policy challenge. The ideal time to purchase is in one''s 50s–early 60s, while still insurable. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
