    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male named Howard is a resident of a skilled nursing facility. The patient''s family reports that the facility has been deciding his daily schedule, restricting his phone calls to family, denying his requests for a different roommate, and refusing to share his care plan with him. Which of the following best describes the regulatory framework protecting this resident''s rights?', '["The Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1987 (OBRA 1987) established a comprehensive Nursing Home Resident Bill of Rights, including the right to be fully informed of one''s care plan, to participate in care decisions, to maintain personal privacy, to communicate freely with family, and to voice grievances without retaliation", "Nursing home residents surrender all individual rights upon admission and must fully comply with facility scheduling and care decisions without the right to any input", "The only right protected under federal law for nursing home residents is the right to receive meals three times daily; all other daily life decisions are at the facility''s discretion", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program is solely responsible for writing nursing home care plans and has no advocacy or rights-protection function", "Resident rights in nursing homes are governed exclusively by individual state laws, and no federal legislation provides any protections to nursing home residents"]'::jsonb, 0, '**OBRA 1987 (Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1987)** is the foundational federal legislation governing nursing home quality and resident rights in Medicare- and Medicaid-certified facilities. **OBRA 1987 Nursing Home Resident Bill of Rights** includes: (1) **Right to be informed** of all rights, services, and charges; (2) **Right to participate** in care planning and decision-making; (3) **Right to privacy** and confidentiality; (4) **Right to communicate** freely with family and the outside world; (5) **Right to grievance** without fear of retaliation; (6) **Right to refuse treatment**; (7) **Right to be free from** physical and chemical restraints used for staff convenience; (8) **Right to personal property and funds**. The **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** program (also established under the Older Americans Act) advocates for resident rights and investigates complaints. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Virginia is a resident of a skilled nursing facility. The patient''s family reports that the facility has been deciding her daily schedule, restricting her phone calls to family, denying her requests for a different roommate, and refusing to share her care plan with her. Which of the following best describes the regulatory framework protecting this resident''s rights?', '["The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program is solely responsible for writing nursing home care plans and has no advocacy or rights-protection function", "The only right protected under federal law for nursing home residents is the right to receive meals three times daily; all other daily life decisions are at the facility''s discretion", "The Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1987 (OBRA 1987) established a comprehensive Nursing Home Resident Bill of Rights, including the right to be fully informed of one''s care plan, to participate in care decisions, to maintain personal privacy, to communicate freely with family, and to voice grievances without retaliation", "Nursing home residents surrender all individual rights upon admission and must fully comply with facility scheduling and care decisions without the right to any input", "Resident rights in nursing homes are governed exclusively by individual state laws, and no federal legislation provides any protections to nursing home residents"]'::jsonb, 2, '**OBRA 1987 (Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1987)** is the foundational federal legislation governing nursing home quality and resident rights in Medicare- and Medicaid-certified facilities. **OBRA 1987 Nursing Home Resident Bill of Rights** includes: (1) **Right to be informed** of all rights, services, and charges; (2) **Right to participate** in care planning and decision-making; (3) **Right to privacy** and confidentiality; (4) **Right to communicate** freely with family and the outside world; (5) **Right to grievance** without fear of retaliation; (6) **Right to refuse treatment**; (7) **Right to be free from** physical and chemical restraints used for staff convenience; (8) **Right to personal property and funds**. The **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** program (also established under the Older Americans Act) advocates for resident rights and investigates complaints. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
