    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old male named Earl is a resident of a skilled nursing facility. The patient''s family reports that the facility has been deciding his daily schedule, restricting his phone calls to family, denying his requests for a different roommate, and refusing to share his care plan with him. Which of the following best describes the regulatory framework protecting this resident''s rights?', '["Resident rights in nursing homes are governed exclusively by individual state laws, and no federal legislation provides any protections to nursing home residents", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program is solely responsible for writing nursing home care plans and has no advocacy or rights-protection function", "The only right protected under federal law for nursing home residents is the right to receive meals three times daily; all other daily life decisions are at the facility''s discretion", "Nursing home residents surrender all individual rights upon admission and must fully comply with facility scheduling and care decisions without the right to any input", "The Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1987 (OBRA 1987) established a comprehensive Nursing Home Resident Bill of Rights, including the right to be fully informed of one''s care plan, to participate in care decisions, to maintain personal privacy, to communicate freely with family, and to voice grievances without retaliation"]'::jsonb, 4, '**OBRA 1987 (Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1987)** is the foundational federal legislation governing nursing home quality and resident rights in Medicare- and Medicaid-certified facilities. **OBRA 1987 Nursing Home Resident Bill of Rights** includes: (1) **Right to be informed** of all rights, services, and charges; (2) **Right to participate** in care planning and decision-making; (3) **Right to privacy** and confidentiality; (4) **Right to communicate** freely with family and the outside world; (5) **Right to grievance** without fear of retaliation; (6) **Right to refuse treatment**; (7) **Right to be free from** physical and chemical restraints used for staff convenience; (8) **Right to personal property and funds**. The **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** program (also established under the Older Americans Act) advocates for resident rights and investigates complaints. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A research team led by Dr. Lillian in Florida presents a study on predictors of nursing home placement in older adults. Which of the following combinations of factors has been most consistently associated with nursing home admission in large epidemiological studies?', '["Advanced age alone (\u226580 years) is the single strongest predictor of nursing home placement, independent of functional status or social support", "High socioeconomic status is the primary predictor of nursing home placement, as wealthy individuals are more likely to choose institutional care than home care", "Living in a rural geographic area is the single most important predictor of nursing home placement, regardless of the patient''s functional status or caregiver availability", "Cognitive impairment (especially dementia), dependence in multiple instrumental and basic ADLs, behavioral disturbances (wandering, nocturnal agitation), caregiver unavailability or burnout, urinary and fecal incontinence, and history of falls", "The presence of mild hypertension and hypercholesterolemia are the most common primary triggers for nursing home placement in the United States"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Epidemiological studies consistently identify a **cluster of biopsychosocial predictors** of nursing home placement: (1) **Cognitive impairment** — dementia is the single strongest clinical predictor; (2) **Functional dependence** — especially ADL dependence (bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring) and IADL loss; (3) **Behavioral symptoms** — wandering, nighttime agitation, aggression; (4) **Incontinence** — both urinary and fecal; (5) **Falls with injury** — leading to immobility; (6) **Caregiver factors** — absence of caregiver, caregiver burnout, small social network; (7) **Prior nursing home admission** — strong predictor of re-admission. Age alone is NOT a predictor when functional status and social support are controlled. Race/ethnicity matters: Black and Hispanic older adults are significantly less likely to use nursing homes than white older adults. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
